WEBVTT

00:00:02.148 --> 00:00:10.754
Clients — Client 360
One golden record per client, shared by every Genesis entity — and everything Genesis does with that client in one place.

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:23.440
Priya, a relationship manager, meets a new client at the branch
Kavya Beeharry, the wife of an existing client, wants to invest with Genesis Wealth

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:27.640
Sign in as Priya Ramdin — Relationship Manager
Relationship manager — she owns the client and the relationship

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:33.760
First, a household for the family
Clients ▸ Households & Groups ▸ New — the family’s value is added up here

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:43.400
A household or corporate group adds up its members’ relationship value
Members join it from their own client record · a corporate group also has a lending limit

00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:50.880
Now the client herself: Clients ▸ Parties ▸ New
One party per person or company — every entity of the group shares it

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:59.240
Identity: the form shows the individual fields for a person
Choose Organisation instead and it asks for the legal name, BRN and sector

00:00:59.240 --> 00:01:07.280
The ID number is personal data: it is stored encrypted and shown masked
Clients must be at least 18 — the date of birth is checked when you save

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:20.640
Contact details, preferred language and channel
Put her in the household you just created

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:32.560
A client can have several addresses and phones
Clients ▸ Addresses & Phones ▸ New — mark one as verified and preferred

00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:41.920
Record her marketing consent: one record per purpose and channel
Clients ▸ Consents ▸ New — Data Protection Act 2017

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:51.640
Marketing campaigns only reach clients with a granted consent
No consent, a withdrawal, “do not contact” or an open complaint suppresses her

00:01:51.640 --> 00:02:03.080
Link related people: spouse, director, shareholder, beneficial owner, guarantor…
Clients ▸ Party Relationships ▸ New

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:15.520
Open her relationship with the entity she will deal with
Clients ▸ Client Relationships ▸ New — one per Genesis entity

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:22.720
The relationship lifecycle: Prospect ▸ Onboarding ▸ Active ▸ Dormant ▸ Closed ▸ Exited
Drag the card to move it

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:27.240
She has agreed to go ahead: move her to Onboarding

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:31.880
Can she become Active straight away?

00:02:31.880 --> 00:02:40.760
No — the move is refused: her KYC is not approved yet
The KYC & Compliance team approves her KYC case; then the move goes through

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:44.280
Closing a relationship asks for the closing date; Exited also asks for a note
These rules are set in the Builder — no code

00:02:47.880 --> 00:02:54.440
Back on her client record: everything is linked
The header shows her email, phone and address

00:02:54.440 --> 00:03:01.960
Related: her relationship, household, address, consent and spouse
Click any row to open it

00:03:01.960 --> 00:03:06.040
The calculated Client 360 figures fill themselves in
KYC: not started yet · Marketing consent: yes

00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:20.960
A week later she calls: no more marketing emails
Open the consent ▸ Edit ▸ status Withdrawn and the withdrawal date

00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:25.560
Marketing consent is now No and she is suppressed from marketing
The latest decision per purpose wins — campaigns skip her automatically

00:03:28.600 --> 00:03:34.920
Now an established client: Christelle Leclézio
Start any client conversation on the client record

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:42.560
ID number and income band are masked for a relationship manager
No Reveal link: her role may not view personal data

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:56.440
Client 360 figures: wealth AUM, fund holdings, total relationship value in MUR and USD
Products held, open applications and complaints, KYC status and AML risk

00:03:56.440 --> 00:04:04.600
Next-best actions open, and the suitable product risk ceiling from her risk profile
Every figure recalculates when a linked record changes

00:04:04.600 --> 00:04:15.080
Related: everything Genesis does with her, across every entity
KYC case, risk profile, fund holdings, next-best actions, consents, meetings, events…

00:04:15.080 --> 00:04:22.320
Rows owned by another desk that you may not open show as “restricted”
Ask Compliance if you need them — nothing is hidden silently

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:33.240
Activity: log the call you just had with her
Calls, meetings, emails and notes stay on the client record

00:04:33.240 --> 00:04:36.240
The call is on her timeline, with who logged it and when

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:40.640
Compliance may view personal data — on demand, and audited
Sign in as the Compliance Officer

00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:50.400
Sign in as Fatima Hossenbux — Compliance Officer
Compliance Officer — her role holds the “view personal data” permission

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:58.800
She also sees the ID number masked — with a Reveal link
Personal data is only shown when someone deliberately asks for it

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:04.040
Revealed — and recorded in the tamper-evident audit trail

00:05:04.040 --> 00:05:07.200
The Audit tab shows who revealed which field, and when
Alongside every change, stage move and approval on the record

00:05:12.380 --> 00:05:22.120
What you learned
The party is the golden record; everything else links to it.
